{
  "dom_id": "step-q154802",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as",
  "worked_solution": "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to all numbers greater than $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191214/assets/9d43b5c54e0beb341f682f44ee4c6d42821a93d45e7d5f0c5576c13e69bff903.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ as\n\n$$\nz\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$",
